Z||000022||mrgone||06-11-2001||02:39 PM||||i thought about it, and i think id rather they not bring it back.<P>i like owning a semi rare car, not everyone knows about them or knows what it is, you dont see them everywhere, and if they started making them GM would probably do it wrong.<P>sure it would be nice to have a new car<P>maybe if they made limited production fieros<BR>or offered a service to update your current fiero to todays technonlogy<P>i always thought it would be a good idea to remanufacture cars exactly like they were years ago<BR>like if they started making americas favourite cars again, EXACTLY as they were when they were originally produced, it would be great. a brand new old car. but i dont think that would be good for current fiero lovers. ||||reg||4
Z||000023||kslish||06-12-2001||12:58 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrgone:<BR><B>i always thought it would be a good idea to remanufacture cars exactly like they were years ago<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Nissan did that a few years ago with a run of original Datsun 240Z's that they remanufactured to like new condition.  I looked at one at a dealership.  It was like $30,000 for the car (which originally cost like 3 grand in 1970) and I heard Nissan still lost a bundle of money by doing it.  It's a labor intensive job.<P>Of course, if one has enough money....I'm sure Ed Parks at the Fiero Factory would be happy to restore a Fiero to showroom condition.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR>See them at <A HREF="http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html</A><BR>Western PA Fiero Club - <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A>||||reg||1
